DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  ssppuu__rruunn()  system  call is used on PowerPC machines that implement
       the Cell Broadband Engine Architecture in order to  access  Synergistic
       Processor  Units (SPUs).  The _f_d argument is a file descriptor returned
       by ssppuu__ccrreeaattee(2) that refers to a specific SPU context.  When the  con‐
       text  gets  scheduled to a physical SPU, it starts execution at the in‐
       struction pointer passed in _n_p_c.

       Execution of SPU code happens  synchronously,  meaning  that  ssppuu__rruunn()
       blocks  while  the SPU is still running.  If there is a need to execute
       SPU code in parallel with other code on either the main  CPU  or  other
       SPUs,  a  new  thread  of  execution must be created first (e.g., using
       pptthhrreeaadd__ccrreeaattee(3)).

       When ssppuu__rruunn() returns, the current value of the SPU program counter is
       written to _n_p_c, so successive calls to ssppuu__rruunn() can use the  same  _n_p_c
       pointer.

       The  _e_v_e_n_t  argument provides a buffer for an extended status code.  If
       the SPU context was created with  the  SSPPUU__CCRREEAATTEE__EEVVEENNTTSS__EENNAABBLLEEDD  flag,
       then  this buffer is populated by the Linux kernel before ssppuu__rruunn() re‐
       turns.

       The status code may be one (or more) of the following constants:

       SSPPEE__EEVVEENNTT__DDMMAA__AALLIIGGNNMMEENNTT
              A DMA alignment error occurred.

       SSPPEE__EEVVEENNTT__IINNVVAALLIIDD__DDMMAA
              An invalid MFC DMA command was attempted.

       SSPPEE__EEVVEENNTT__SSPPEE__DDAATTAA__SSTTOORRAAGGEE
              A DMA storage error occurred.

       SSPPEE__EEVVEENNTT__SSPPEE__EERRRROORR
              An illegal instruction was executed.

       NULL is a valid value for the _e_v_e_n_t argument.  In this case, the events
       will not be reported to the calling process.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       On success, ssppuu__rruunn() returns the value of the _s_p_u___s_t_a_t_u_s register.  On
       failure, it returns -1 and sets _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the error.

       The _s_p_u___s_t_a_t_u_s register value is a bit mask of status codes and option‐
       ally a 14‐bit code returned from the ssttoopp‐‐aanndd‐‐ssiiggnnaall instruction on the
       SPU.  The bit masks for the status codes are:

       00xx0022   SPU was stopped by a ssttoopp‐‐aanndd‐‐ssiiggnnaall instruction.

       00xx0044   SPU was stopped by a hhaalltt instruction.

       00xx0088   SPU is waiting for a channel.

       00xx1100   SPU is in single‐step mode.

       00xx2200   SPU has tried to execute an invalid instruction.

       00xx4400   SPU has tried to access an invalid channel.

       00xx33ffffff00000000
              The bits masked with this value contain the code returned from a
              ssttoopp‐‐aanndd‐‐ssiiggnnaall instruction.  These bits are valid only  if  the
              0x02 bit is set.

       If  ssppuu__rruunn()  has  not  returned  an error, one or more bits among the
       lower eight ones are always set.

